    "heading": "What the router may tell it",
    "url": "components/fleet-agent.html#what-the-router-may-tell-it",
    "text": "Identifiers and bounded integers. The socket carries no image name, no host address, no file path, no container name, no Docker flag, no shell fragment, and no URL — only opaque deployment-id, artifact-id, host-id, and lease-id tokens both sides hold from their own configuration. The agent maintains its own allowlist (fleet.example.json) mapping each identifier to a host, an SSH destination, and a command. The router cannot extend it. The goal is specific: a fully compromised router cannot cause arbitrary code to run on a slave. It can reorder declared deployments; it cannot introduce one. An unrecognised identifier is refused with ERR unknown_deployment. The one exception is ENROLL (specification 26.8): the router may offer hosts that an administrator approved and a second person published, with their address, SSH login, host key, data directory and accelerators. The agent adopts an offer only when its own file sets enrollment.accept, only for addresses inside its own enrollment.cidrs, pins each host key, and gives an enrolled host no deployment. A hand-written host of the same identifier wins. See Enrolled hosts."
