    "heading": "What this reference implementation does not do",
    "url": "components/fleet-agent.html#what-this-reference-implementation-does-not-do",
    "text": "Stated so the gaps are not mistaken for controls: Fetch resumption is at layer granularity, not byte granularity. FETCH retries within the router's deadline, and docker pull skips layers already in the host's content store — so each attempt resumes from what the last one finished. A layer interrupted mid-transfer is re-fetched. An artifact published as one enormous layer therefore gains nothing from this, which is a property of how it was built rather than of this agent. No Windows-host support. Four of the six machines are Windows hosts whose SSH lands in WSL, where powershell.exe interop reaches the Windows side. Driving that is entirely the agent's concern — the router must never learn that some hosts need it — but this reference agent does not implement it. No forced command= check. It is an obligation above, configured on the slave rather than by this program. The host kit (host/install.sh) installs one on an enrolled machine; a machine added by hand has whatever its operator configured, and an agent key without the restriction has a much larger blast radius. Image relay always goes through this machine. COPY never connects two slaves directly, even when they could reach each other, so every byte crosses the agent machine's link twice and needs room in its spool. Only images move. A container's state directory is not copied, and a running container is never migrated. Observation is serial. Each host is probed in turn on the observing thread. With five hosts and a five-second interval this is comfortable; with fifty it would not be."
