    "text": "Specification 26.12. COPY <artifact> <source> <destination> <deadline-ms> <image-id> moves an image between two hosts in this table, through this machine — no registry, and no connection between the two slaves. Enable it with: \"transfer\": { \"spool_dir\": \"/var/lib/hypellm-agent/spool\", \"max_spool_bytes\": 107374182400 } Without the block every COPY is refused copy_disabled. A copy: streams docker save <image> from the source into <spool_dir>/<activation>.tar, killed the moment it exceeds max_spool_bytes, the router's deadline, or a CANCEL; hashes the archive: the configuration must hash to the image ID, each layer — decompressed if gzip — to its rootfs.diff_ids entry, and an OCI blob to its own name. Both layouts docker save writes are read; an archive holding two images, an escaping link, or a zstd layer (which nothing in Python's standard library can decompress, so nothing could check) is refused; streams a fresh archive, rebuilt from exactly the verified configuration and layers and tagged with this table's reference, into docker load on the destination — never the archive it received, whose other members a newer docker load might prefer; checks that the destination now reports the image ID, and deletes the spool. Any mismatch ends the copy failed checksum_mismatch with nothing loaded. The image ID is the one non-identifier on the socket; it can only narrow, and when this table pins image_id on the artifact a router asking for another is refused checksum_mismatch. A COPY and a FETCH share the one-transfer-per-destination rule. An artifact whose image is a digest reference (name@sha256:…) cannot be copied (copy_needs_tag): nothing would be left to check it by name afterwards. Observation reports, per artifact on each host, present, verified — now meaning the image carries the registry digest declared for it — and the image_id the host reports, which the router compares with the checksum it keeps."
