    "text": "Outbound HTTPS goes through a platform TLS helper over a Unix socket, named by settings tls_helper_socket. The wire protocol is deliberately tiny (crates/hypellm-net/src/helper.rs): → CONNECT <host> <port> <sni>\\n ← OK\\n then the socket carries the TLS session's plaintext ← ERR <code>\\n and the socket closes The router sends only a host, a port, and an SNI name, all of which came from configuration — never a URL, a path, or a header. The helper is inside the trusted computing base: it validates certificates, selects ciphers, and should enforce its own destination allowlist. Its error codes are sanitised to 64 characters of [A-Za-z0-9_-] before they reach a log or an error body. If tls_helper_socket is not configured and any provider declares scheme=https, startup fails with StartupError::Unreachable (exit 2) listing each endpoint that \"requires outbound TLS, but no tls_helper_socket is configured\". The router never silently downgrades. The reference helper is tls-helper/: the platform's OpenSSL through Python's ssl module, a deny-by-default (sni, port) allowlist, and a re-check of the router's pinned address. The pod in k8s/hypellm.yaml runs it as a sidecar on /run/hypellm-tls/tls.sock, idle until the settings record names it: tls_helper_socket=/run/hypellm-tls/tls.sock Its component page has a complete DeepSeek example — credential, provider, target and alias — and what each refusal code means."
