    "page": "Router specification",
    "group": "Design & security",
    "heading": "26.8 Enrollment",
    "url": "router-specification.html#268-enrollment",
    "text": "A machine joins the fleet in four steps, and is unavailable until the last one. Issue. An operator holding fleet_enroll mints a one-time token for a host identifier and an agent (POST /admin/v1/fleet/enrollments). The token MUST be at least 256 bits from the OS random source, shown once, and stored only as a domain-separated digest. It MUST expire (default 30 minutes, at most 24 hours). Minting MUST be refused unless settings fleet_enroll_url names an https origin and the agent's record declares ssh_public_key. Open enrollments are bounded. Register. The machine runs the install script served at GET /fleet/v1/install.sh on the management listener, over the https origin. The script MUST check its dependencies before contacting the router, MUST show every change it will make and obtain approval before making any, and MUST NOT install a package, start a service or use privilege escalation. It installs the agent's key in authorized_keys restricted to a forced command that parses the requested command without a shell and refuses anything outside a fixed list. It then reports, with the token, its LAN address, architecture, accelerators, memory, data directory, SSH host key and a device public key (POST /fleet/v1/enroll:complete). Approve. An operator holding fleet_enroll and edit_policy approves the registration. Approval MUST create a policy draft adding the machine's host and accelerator records and MUST NOT publish it. Publish and offer. Publication follows §15.4 unchanged, including its approver rule. Only once a host is in the active configuration does the router offer it to its agent (ENROLL, §26.2), carrying the pinned address, login, host key, data directory and accelerators. The offer MAY be sent while the digests disagree, because a newly published host is what makes them disagree; it MUST be delivered on a connection of its own on which no other verb is sent. The two /fleet/v1/ endpoints are reachable without a session or key, and are therefore held to more than the rest of the listener: both MUST answer 404 when enrollment is not configured; a request carrying Origin MUST be refused; a used, rejected, expired and unknown token MUST be indistinguishable to the caller; failed presentations MUST be throttled; and every registered field MUST be validated against a closed alphabet before it is stored, because it later reaches a known_hosts file, an SSH destination and a container volume path. Every issue, registration, approval, rejection and revocation MUST be audited. A revoked enrollment is withdrawn from the agent's next offer; its records remain until a draft removes them. The device key is stored and fingerprinted for future authenticated LAN requests from the slave. Its signatures, when such requests exist, MUST be verified by the local verifier (§9.1), never in the router."
